<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sopron</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Ödenburg</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Sopron, in German Ödenburg, is a city in north-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a> close to the Austrian border, built on the site of the Roman town of Scarbantia. Although the peace treaties after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a> assigned the surrounding region to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burgenland">Burgenland</a>, a plebiscite in December 1921 kept the city and nearby villages in Hungary, earning it the title of most loyal town. Its medieval and baroque old town centres on the Fire Tower.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sopron.jpg" alt="Sopron" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Thaler · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Storno-h%C3%A1z_T%C5%B1ztorony_%C3%A9s_V%C3%A1rosh%C3%A1za.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>An <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iron-age">Iron Age</a> hilltop settlement with a burial ground lay near Sopron-Várhely. Under Roman rule the city of Scarbantia stood here, and the site of its forum is now the main square. By the time Hungarians arrived the city was in ruins. From the 9th to the 11th centuries they strengthened the old Roman walls and built a castle, and the city takes its Hungarian name from a castle steward named Suprun. The city was mentioned as important in 1153. In 1273 King Otakar II of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bohemia">Bohemia</a> occupied the castle, but the city opened its gates to King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ladislaus-iv-of-hungary">Ladislaus IV of Hungary</a>, who raised it to the rank of free royal town.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> ravaged the city in 1529 without occupying it, and many Hungarians fled there from occupied areas. A fire destroyed the city in 1676, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baroque">Baroque</a> buildings replaced the medieval ones over the following decades. After the compromise of 1867 until 1918 it was part of the Habsburg-ruled Kingdom of Hungary, with the dual name Sopron and Ödenburg. In 1910 its 33,931 inhabitants were 51 per cent German and 44.3 per cent Hungarian.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>After the breakup of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austro-hungarian-empire">Austro-Hungarian Empire</a> the German-inhabited parts of four western Hungarian counties were first awarded to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-saint-germain-en-laye-1919">Treaty of Saint-Germain-en-Laye</a> in 1919. After local unrest and Italian mediation in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice-protocol">Venice Protocol</a>, a controversial plebiscite on 14 December 1921 decided the status of Sopron and eight surrounding villages, and 65 per cent voted for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a>. Since then the city has been called Civitas Fidelissima, the Most Loyal City, and the anniversary is a city holiday. The western parts of the counties formed the Austrian state of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burgenland">Burgenland</a>.</p><p>Sopron was bombed several times in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">World War II</a>, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-army">Red Army</a> captured it on 1 April 1945. On 19 August 1989 it was the site of the Pan-European Picnic on the border with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a>, which over 600 East Germans used to escape to the West and which helped pave the way for the mass flight that preceded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fall-of-the-berlin-wall">fall of the Berlin Wall</a>.</p><p>Walls and foundations from Roman times remain in the old town, together with Medieval, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/renaissance">Renaissance</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baroque">Baroque</a> buildings, among them the Esterházy Palace and the Firewatch Tower. Sopron is also a wine-producing region, known for Kékfrankos red and Traminer white wines, and its street signs are in Hungarian and German.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Sopron” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Under Italian mediation the Venice Protocol of October 1921 agreed a plebiscite in <b>Sopron</b>, and the irregulars withdrew in November.</blockquote><a href="/state/banate-of-leitha">Banate of Leitha</a></li></ul></section>
